\section{Executive Summary}

What has been done since my proposal defense: no actual results or work for the benchmarking project were part of my proposal defense, so every tangible part of the benchmarking project is new. In brief, I have done the following:

\begin{enumerate}

\item \textbf{gRNA assignment comparison:} \texttt{SCEPTRE}, \texttt{pertpy}, \texttt{CLEANSER}, and \texttt{crispat} have been evaluated and compared on both simulated and real datasets. 

\begin{enumerate}
\item I developed a nextflow pipeline to reproducibly parallelize gRNA assignment over dataset-method pairs, with metrics tracking, and GPU usage for \texttt{pertpy}

\item I created an original mechanism for simulating gRNA matrices with covariate effects and known ground truth

\item  I compared and contrasted all 4 of these methods on 2 simulated datasets and 2 real datasets, both statistically and computationally, as well as running the \texttt{sceptre-pipeline} on both real datasets
\end{enumerate}

\item \textbf{Association analysis comparison:} so far, \texttt{SCEPTRE}, \texttt{Mixscale}, and \texttt{FR-Perturb} have been evaluated and compared on real datasets for on-target performance
\begin{enumerate}
\item I created on-target downsamples of two real datasets, and developed a nextflow pipeline to reproducibly compare and contrast all 3 of these methods statistically using pre-determined guide assignments
\end{enumerate}


\end{enumerate}


Major steps remaining:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Association: negative control analysis

\item Association: computational benchmarking

\item Possibly adding more datasets and methods

\item Writing the paper, thesis, and thesis defense
\end{enumerate}


\section{Detailed Report}

I will now give a review of the key findings so far.

\subsection{Guide Assignment}

For this project, we are currently comparing 4 methods on 2 simulated datasets, and 2 real datasets. 

The methods are as follows:

\begin{enumerate}

\item \texttt{pertpy}'s \texttt{assign\_mixture\_model()}: a Poisson-Gaussian mixture model fit via MCMC to the log-transformed counts. 

\item \texttt{CLEANSER}: either a Poisson-NB or NB-NB mixture, fit to the raw counts via MCMC, with a scaling factor for total library size. 

\item \texttt{crispat}'s \texttt{ga\_poisson\_gauss()}: a Poisson-Gaussian mixture fit to the log-transformed counts. MAP estimates are obtained via stochastic variational inference.

\item \texttt{SCEPTRE}: a latent variable Poisson GLM, fit via a modified EM algorithm, controlling for some guide covariates and possibly batch

\end{enumerate}


The datasets are as follows:

\begin{enumerate}

\item Simulated: two datasets with 100k cells were generated, where each guide is an independent sample of the same process. Covariate effects learned from Replogle-RD7 were included. The fraction of perturbed cells was set to either 1.5\% or 0.5\%. Currently only a large perturbation effect size is used.

\item Real: Replogle-RD7 (possibly downsampled to the first 2500 guides and first 50,000 cells) and Gasperini (also possibly downsampled to the first 2500 guides and first 50,000 cells)

\end{enumerate}

\subsubsection{Simulation results}

Figure \ref{fig:sims-umis} gives a comparison of the real and simulated data. Table \ref{tab:sims-results} gives the comparison of each method with the ground truth, while Table \ref{tab:sims-compute} shows the total memory use and runtime for each method on each dataset. 

\texttt{SCEPTRE} is not quite the best in terms of these metrics, but is also never far from it. \texttt{CLEANSER} and \texttt{pertpy} both have the potential to fail. \texttt{SCEPTRE} strongly wins in terms of computational metrics.

