\subsubsection{Results on real data}

The downsampled Replogle and Gasperini datasets are used here, so they are denoted \texttt{medium}. Both have 50k cells and 2500 guides. Figure \ref{fig:assignment-real-jaccard} shows the agreement between each pair of methods on each guide. All but \texttt{pertpy} generally agree. Table \ref{tab:assignment-real-avgs} shows the estimated experimental MOI and the average number of cells assigned to each guide. We again see \texttt{pertpy} as an outlier. Table \ref{tab:assignment-real-compute} shows the computational results for these 4 methods, as well as for the \texttt{sceptre-pipeline}. \texttt{SCEPTRE} and the pipeline are by far the fastest and most memory efficient.

Overall we see a picture of \texttt{SCEPTRE} being nearly the best method statistically, and by far the best computationally, so for large-scale guide assignment it is a very strong choice.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=1.2\linewidth]{figures/assignment-real-jaccard.pdf}
  \caption{A comparison of the Jaccard similarities between the assignments made for each guide by each method pair. We can see that \texttt{SCEPTRE}, \texttt{crispat}, and \texttt{CLEANSER} generally agree, but \texttt{pertpy} can be quite different.}
  \label{fig:assignment-real-jaccard}
\end{figure}



