\subsection{Association: positive control analysis}

Next, we turn to association analysis. For this part of the project, we are currently comparing 3 methods on 2 real datasets.

The methods are as follows:

\begin{enumerate}

\item \texttt{FR-Perturb}: An approach based on sparse factorization. Both the resampling and skew-t variants are included. 

\item \texttt{Mixscale}: Inference is done via a NB regression using Mixscape-style scores as weights to account for perturbation effectiveness. Low MOI only.

\item \texttt{SCEPTRE}

\end{enumerate}


The datasets are as follows:

\begin{enumerate}

\item \textbf{Replogle-RD7 on-target}: the Replogle-RD7 data, downsampled to around 210k cells, with around 1600 on-target genes, corresponding to around 1800 guides. Only on-target genes were included where every guide for them had at least 20 cells expressing it. Cell expressions were obtained via the \texttt{sceptre-pipeline}.

\item \textbf{Gasperini on-target}: the Gasperini data, downsampled to around 185k cells, with almost 400 on-target genes, corresponding to around 850 guides. Cell expressions were obtained via the \texttt{sceptre-pipeline}.

\end{enumerate}

Table \ref{tab:pc-method-results} gives some results for each dataset-method pair. SCEPTRE has by far the smallest p-values. Next, Table \ref{tab:pc-method-pair-results} shows that SCEPTRE and Mixscale give very similar rankings to the on-target genes, and the two FR-Perturb methods strongly agree with each other as well, but there is very little agreement between FR-Perturb and the other methods. Finally, Figure \ref{fig:pc-p-value-violins} shows the distribution of the $-\log_{10}$ p-values for each method. FR-Perturb can be seen to be much more concentrated on large p-values.

